WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.590
A student’s project had a

2
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:03.930
lovely number in it.

3
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.960
Seventy-three percent of homes in

4
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.720
our town have running water, “from

5
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.220
a government report”.

6
00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.310
I asked to see the report.

7
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.320
It doesn’t exist: a chatbot made it up.

8
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.270
Today: why AI sometimes makes

9
00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.310
things up, and how to check.

10
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.130
Remember how a chatbot writes: it

11
00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.910
predicts what’s likely, it

12
00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.290
doesn’t check what’s true.

13
00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.550
When it doesn’t know, it rarely says so.

14
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.520
It fills the gap with

15
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.140
something that sounds right.

16
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.860
Even chatbots that search the web

17
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.480
can mix up what they find.

18
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.570
When AI states something false as if it

19
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.250
were true, we call it a hallucination.

20
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.660
It can invent facts, numbers and dates,

21
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.680
quotes nobody said, and sources that

22
00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.900
don’t exist: books, links, reports.

23
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.010
You can’t check every word,

24
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:03.530
so check what matters.

25
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.940
Exact numbers, dates and names.

26
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.940
Quotes and sources.

27
00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:10.890
Anything about your own

28
00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.290
country, where it learned less.

29
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.180
And anything you’ll hand in or share.

30
00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.550
It’s like a classmate who always

31
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.450
answers first and always sounds sure.

32
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.000
They’re often right.

33
00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:25.570
But before you copy their answer

34
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.090
into your notes for the exam,

35
00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:28.610
you’d check it in the book.

36
00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:30.470
Quick check!

37
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.180
A chatbot gives you a date for

38
00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.340
your history project.

39
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.630
What should you do with it?

40
00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.050
Check it in two trusted sources.

41
00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:45.150
Your textbook, your teacher, or a

42
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.950
trusted website, like a ministry, a

43
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.590
university or a well-known news site.

44
00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.470
Asking the chatbot again isn’t

45
00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.350
checking: it can repeat its

46
00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:56.890
mistake just as confidently.

47
00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.340
Two sources that agree, and don’t just

48
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.980
copy each other, are worth far more than

49
00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:03.220
one.

50
00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.930
It’s not only text.

51
00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.580
AI can make photos, videos and voices

52
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.420
that look and sound real: a flood that

53
00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.760
never happened, a famous footballer

54
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.280
saying something he never said.

55
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.600
Before you share, ask: who made

56
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.640
this, where’s the original, and do

57
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.320
trusted sources report it?

58
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.120
In the app, you’ll check an AI answer

59
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.560
about the river Nile, claim by claim.

60
00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.770
Mark each one correct,

61
00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.250
wrong, or check a source.

62
00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:39.170
One claim is a confident mistake,

63
00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.690
and one is completely invented.

64
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:43.910
Your turn.

65
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.990
A shocking video of a famous person

66
00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:48.250
lands in your class group.

67
00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.130
What do you do first?

68
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:54.190
Check before you share: videos

69
00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.950
can be made by AI too.

70
00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.580
Remember: AI can invent facts,

71
00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.100
numbers, quotes and sources.

72
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.560
Check what matters in two trusted sources.

73
00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.090
And if you can’t find it

74
00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.570
anywhere trusted, don’t use it.

75
00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:15.390
Try it yourself: take an AI answer, or a

76
00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.290
message forwarded in your family group.

77
00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:19.410
Pick three facts from it.

78
00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.250
Check each one in your

79
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:22.950
textbook, or with a teacher.

80
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:24.480
How many held up?

81
00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.040
If one didn’t, tell the

82
00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.740
person who sent it, kindly.
